# shoulder-anchor.xrs - does turning the HEAD move the shoulder, and if so, why?
#
# The reported bug (end of s73, never chased): "head rotation moves each arm's
# shoulder position."
#
# THIS IS A TEST A HUMAN CANNOT RUN, for the same reason offhand-swivel.xrs
# gives about the off hand. The question is whether the shoulder moves *while
# the controllers are still*, and nobody can hold two controllers still to the
# precision the answer needs - a few centimetres of real hand drift is larger
# than the effect. The simulator parks both controllers on exact poses and never
# touches them again, so every millimetre the shoulder-to-hand distance changes
# afterwards was caused by the HEAD and nothing else.
#
# THE ORACLE IS THE LOG, NOT THE CAPTURES. The s74a SHOULDER probe prints the
# numbers directly, so this file needs no image diff and inherits none of the
# "BS1 scenery moves on its own" traps that make offhand-swivel.xrs so careful:
#
#   grep -a SHOULDER %LOCALAPPDATA%\BioshockVR\bioshockvr.log
#
# Each line carries: the shoulder in WORLD, base[XYZ], camYaw, the derived
# bodyYaw, the drive residual, the recenter yaw, and shoulder-to-hand against
# the arm's reach. SHOULDER is deliberately outside the s74b probe gate, so it
# needs no `vrbones probes on` - and leaving the gate shut is what keeps the log
# readable here.
#
# ================== WHY THERE ARE TWO PHASES ==================
#
# The hypothesis this file exists to test is that the shoulder is NOT leaking
# the head at all. bones.cpp anchors it to (camYaw - driveYawOffsetRad), which
# is meant to be the BODY's yaw - but the body-follows-head transfer ships at
# instant 1:1 (body.cpp: g_armed true, g_ratePerSec 0, g_deadzoneDeg 0), so in
# steady state that residual is ~0 and the body yaw simply IS the head yaw. On
# that reading the shoulder is faithfully following a body that snapped to the
# head, and the fix belongs in the transfer, not in the arm solve.
#
# One phase cannot separate those. Two can, because `vrbody off` disarms the
# transfer and nothing else:
#
#   PHASE A - transfer ARMED (the shipping default)
#   PHASE B - transfer OFF   (`vrbody off`)
#
# HOW TO READ THE PAIR. Compare the SHOULDER lines across each sweep:
#
#   A: bodyYaw tracks camYaw and the shoulder moves
#   B: bodyYaw HOLDS while camYaw moves, and the shoulder HOLDS
#       -> CONFIRMED. The transfer carries the head into the shoulder. There is
#          no arm bug; do not touch solve_arm.
#
#   B: bodyYaw HOLDS while camYaw moves, and the shoulder MOVES ANYWAY
#       -> a genuine leak downstream of the yaw. base[XYZ] is the next suspect,
#          and the base(...) field on the same line already shows whether it is
#          the one moving.
#
#   Either phase: sh->hand CHANGES
#       -> the arm is really stretching, and by exactly that many UU. If it
#          holds, shoulder and hand are orbiting the same centre rigidly and
#          what the tester saw is a percept, not a geometry error.
#
# sh->hand is the discriminator. The shoulder MOVING in world proves nothing on
# its own - the hand orbits the same centre and the whole rig is allowed to yaw
# with the body. What the tester can actually see is the shoulder moving
# relative to a hand that did not move.
#
# ================== PRECONDITION. DO NOT SKIP. ==================
#
# THE SIM HOLDING A POSE DOES NOT MEAN THE MOD IS READING IT. That trap
# invalidated a whole run on 2026-08-30 that had already been reported as a
# reproduction (see offhand-swivel.xrs). Here it is cheap to rule out, because
# the probe prints camYaw itself:
#
#   .\tools\xrsim-cmd.ps1 "head rot  60 0 0"
#   .\tools\xrsim-cmd.ps1 "head rot -60 0 0"
#   grep -a SHOULDER %LOCALAPPDATA%\BioshockVR\bioshockvr.log | tail -4
#
# camYaw MUST differ by roughly 120 deg between those two. If it does not move,
# the mod is not reading the simulated head and everything below is scenery.
#
# A WEAPON MUST BE EQUIPPED AND BOTH HANDS DRAWN. solve_arm() returns early
# while the arm reference is invalid or either arm is collapsed, and an
# empty-handed or menu-state run prints no SHOULDER lines at all. An empty grep
# means "there was no arm to measure", not "the shoulder held still".
#
# THE 400 ms THROTTLE IS WALL CLOCK, not frames. The @frames counts below are
# generous so that each pose yields at least two lines per role and side; if a
# pose produced only one, raise them rather than trusting a single sample.
#
# THE SLEW CAP IS REAL. g_maxDegPerSec is 180, so a 60 deg head step takes about
# a third of a second to transfer even at rate 0. The settle counts below cover
# it - but the first line after each step may catch the transient, which is
# useful in itself: driveYaw is non-zero exactly while the body is catching up.

reset
@mod vrstereo on
@mod vrhands on; vrhands mode brvr
@frames 60
@assert projectionViews eq 2

# Park BOTH controllers and never touch them again. Everything after this point
# that moves the shoulder-to-hand distance was the head's doing.
hand l grip pose -0.25 1.30 -0.40 0 0 0
hand r grip pose  0.25 1.30 -0.45 0 0 0
head rot 0 0 0
@frames 150
@shot shoulder_A_yaw00
@assert layersLastFrame ge 3

# ---------------- PHASE A: the transfer as shipped (armed, 1:1) -------------
head rot 30 0 0
@frames 150
@shot shoulder_A_yawP30

head rot 60 0 0
@frames 150
@shot shoulder_A_yawP60

head rot 0 0 0
@frames 150
@shot shoulder_A_return

head rot -60 0 0
@frames 150
@shot shoulder_A_yawM60

head rot 0 0 0
@frames 150

# ---------------- PHASE B: the same sweep, transfer disarmed ----------------
# `vrbody off` disarms the body-follows-head yaw transfer and nothing else, so
# the ONLY difference between the two phases is whether the body was allowed to
# follow. That is what makes the pair an experiment rather than two runs.
@mod vrbody off
@frames 150
@shot shoulder_B_yaw00

head rot 30 0 0
@frames 150
@shot shoulder_B_yawP30

head rot 60 0 0
@frames 150
@shot shoulder_B_yawP60

head rot 0 0 0
@frames 150
@shot shoulder_B_return

head rot -60 0 0
@frames 150
@shot shoulder_B_yawM60

head rot 0 0 0
@frames 150

# Leave the transfer as it was found - it is the shipping default and a sticky
# `off` would silently change the next run in this session. `vrstereo on` is
# sticky for the same reason offhand-swivel.xrs arms it explicitly.
@mod vrbody on
